-- title:
--   Torsion of Pic⁰ over a locally finite constant field
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, let $\ell$ be a prime number and suppose $K$ has characteristic $\ell$. Assume (`halg`) that every $a \in K$ satisfies $a^{\ell^n} = a$ for some $n > 0$, and (`hfg`) that there is an $x \in F$ transcendental over $K$ such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Assume further that `IsCurveOver K F` holds, i.e. every nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor (a finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued function on the places of $F/K$, a place being a proper valuation subring of $F$ containing $\operatorname{im}(K \to F)$ and whose ring is a principal ideal ring) recording the orders $v(f)$ and of total degree $0$, each residue field of a place is finite-dimensional over $K$, and the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega_{F/K}$ is free of rank $1$ over $F$; and assume `ConstantsAreBase K F`, i.e. the Riemann–Roch space $L(0)$ of the zero divisor is exactly the image of $K$ in $F$. Then for every class $c$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0(K,F)$ — the group of degree-zero divisors modulo the principal ones — there exists $m > 0$ with $m \cdot c = 0$.
--
--   This is the statement, due in the classical setting to F. K. Schmidt, that the degree-zero divisor class group of a one-variable function field whose constant field is algebraic over $\mathbb{F}_\ell$ is a torsion group. It is used in the treatment of the modular curve function field, where it yields finite additive order of the relevant degree-zero classes, as in [`ModularCurve.isOfFinAddOrder_jZeroC_of_isAlgebraic`](thm.html#ModularCurve.isOfFinAddOrder_jZeroC_of_isAlgebraic) and [`ModularCurve.fTorsionFor_pic0_residueField_modularFunctionFieldC`](thm.html#ModularCurve.fTorsionFor_pic0_residueField_modularFunctionFieldC).
